        "bodyTr": "The Democratic Party and the Defense Ministry held a government-party coordination meeting yesterday to announce a revised basic plan for establishing the Korea Military Academy (KMA) through the integration of the Army, Navy, and Air Force academies. The plan calls for first- and fourth-year students to receive integrated education at a newly established KMA in Jaun-dae, Daejeon, while second- and third-year courses will be led by each branch. The Army will relocate the Seoul-based Army Academy from Taereung to the Chungcheong region, while the Navy and Air Force will continue using their current facilities in Jinhae and Cheongju, respectively. This revision was made in response to criticism of \"a navy without the sea\" and \"an air force without a runway,\" departing from the original plan to conduct four years of integrated education at Jaun-dae. Once the Military Academy Act is amended according to the revised plan, a newly established Faculty of Army-Navy-Air Force will be created while the three separate service academies will disappear. Military authorities cite preparation for future warfare as the main rationale for integration and explain that relocation is necessary due to insufficient training facilities caused by urbanization around the Army Academy. Nevertheless, questions remain. First, the military's explanation of how future warfare will evolve is insufficiently specific. Without accurate diagnosis, effective solutions cannot be achieved. Moreover, the integration plan has caused continued confusion, moving from discussion of \"first- and second-year integration\" in April to \"four-year full integration\" in July and now to this revised version. It is unavoidable to point out that the restructuring of an 80-year-old military academy education system has been subjected to piecemeal solutions without sufficient deliberation and consensus-building. The Defense Ministry now claims it will \"conduct research projects through specialized institutions and discuss detailed curricula with various experts,\" but this merely demonstrates that the military has already predetermined the relocation of the Army Academy and is pushing through the policy accordingly. Given this situation, suspicions persist that the KMA establishment is about \"erasing the Army Academy\" stemming from the involvement of some Army Academy graduates in the December 12-13 martial law incident. Among countries maintaining over 300,000 standing troops, only a handful such as Thailand and Venezuela operate integrated military academies. North Korea, which openly threatens us, further subdivides officer training by separately operating special forces and artillery military schools even within its army alone. Changes to the system for cultivating military leaders to guide our armed forces must proceed with public consensus, excluding political motivations of any faction and focusing solely on strengthening national security capabilities.",
