      "byline": "EDITORIAL",
      "body": "Excessive KFTC penalties fuel litigation, ultimately burden taxpayers, businesses The Korea Fair Trade Commission’s proposed increase in its litigation budget sparked controversy at a subcommittee meeting of the National Assembly’s Special Committee on Budget and Accounts on Monday. The commission requested 5.1 billion won ($3.84 million) for next year’s litigation budget, 34 percent above this year’s 3.8 billion won. The committee’s senior expert adviser recommended a 1.1 billion won cut, but the commission insisted on retaining the full amount. The commission imposes fines on companies for unfair practices such as collusion, many of which are subsequently challenged in administrative lawsuits. Defending them inevitably entails litigation costs. Opposition lawmakers argued that the commission’s excessive use of fines under the current administration had triggered more lawsuits, driving up litigation expenses. The commission imposed more than 4 trillion won in fines in six major price-fixing cases this year, including those involving government bonds and flour. The total of 4.05 trillion won is more than double the 1.97 trillion imposed from 2022 to 2024. Fines approved in the first half of this year alone totaled 1.75 trillion won, five times last year’s full-year figure. In a government bond bidding-rigging case announced this month, the commission imposed a staggering 1.62 trillion won in fines on 15 financial institutions serving as primary dealers. It is the commission’s largest-ever fine in a single case. The institutions have vowed to challenge the penalties in court. Earlier, the commission imposed a staggering 1.81 trillion won in fines after uncovering price-fixing in three food ingredient markets: sugar (396 billion won), flour (671 billion won) and starch and starch syrup (747.6 billion won). Disputes over fine calculations have accompanied every major price-fixing announcement. The commission also fined food delivery and commerce platforms Baemin and Coupang Eats more than 600 billion won for allegedly abusing their market dominance to demand preferential treatment. Coupang Eats alone was ultimately fined 300.8 billion won, more than seven times the amount estimated during the initial review. Meanwhile, companies continue to secure reductions in fines by challenging the commission’s decisions in court. The Seoul High Court overturned in full a 234.9 billion won fine imposed on Samsung affiliates for steering food service contracts to Samsung Welstory. The Supreme Court upheld a ruling that canceled 36.5 billion won of a 60.8 billion won fine imposed on Hoban Construction. Since 2021, the commission has refunded 191.7 billion won in fines, with taxpayers footing the bill for interest on those refunds. Rep. Song Eon-seok of the People Power Party said the rate at which courts granted requests to suspend enforcement of the commission’s sanctions had risen from 64.3 percent in 2024 to 73.5 percent in 2025 and 79.5 percent as of the end of September this year. Even when courts reduce excessive fines, companies suffer lasting damage from the time and money spent contesting them. When the government loses, it must also pay interest on the refunds, adding to the burden on the state treasury. The national economy would be better served by rational standards for calculating appropriate fines than by a bigger litigation budget. Facing potentially crippling penalties, companies rush to major law firms that have recruited former commission officials. According to reports, 86 former Fair Trade Commission officials have joined the country’s 10 largest law firms over the past decade. The commission can hardly escape suspicions that it is using its market-protection mandate to squeeze businesses and ultimately enrich former officials. The commission must establish a reasonable framework for calculating fines that the market can accept and ensure that penalties are proportionate. It should stop imposing excessive fines that end up being contested in court. How will it answer critics who say that, in the end, only law firms stand to profit? Over half million students take Suneung amid AI cheating concerns The annual college entrance exam is underway nationwide Thursday as authorities make all-out efforts to crack down on cheating involving smart devices and artificial intelligence. Trump, Xi, Putin set to hold historic summit; US-N. Korea meeting uncertain Man confesses to killing teen girl over son’s statutory rape case Why Korea? BTS, skincare and K-drama, tourists say From paychecks to parcels, a guide to sending things home from Korea From ballads to burgers: John Park finds viral fame with McDonald’s rap Stars Up Close Samsung, LG Display bet on China's gaming OLED boom China Factor The Ultrarich Lifestyle of the ultrarich Stars Up Close A spotlight on Korean artists who rose to prominence in unexpected ways, and moments behind their breakthroughs K-parenting 101 Dive into the world of Korean parenting Herald Interview A series of in-depth interviews. [Editorial] Nvidia without Huang [Editorial] High-tech cheating [Lee Kyong-hee] Kim Jong-un shouldn’t miss his best chance [Editorial] The trillion-dollar test [Grace Kao] Lee Soo-man, A2O and 1980s synthwave Why phones across Korea blared emergency alerts Thursday, even on silent Why do people cheat? The psychology behind Korea's AI exam scandal Why Korean actors are playing European royalty in K-drama's new fantasy trend Jung Ho-yeon joins Zendaya, Anya Taylor-Joy in Bond girl speculation A $1,500 ski outfit? A look into Kim Ju-ae’s slope style",
